# All In Moderation — environment template (Phase 02B.1)
#
# Copy to `.env` and adjust. Every value below has a safe default in src/runtime/config.ts, so an
# empty file boots. Configuration is validated eagerly at start-up: a bad value fails the process
# on boot rather than on the first customer message.
#
# Secrets are deliberately NOT read from here. Channel credentials are resolved by reference
# through the `SecretResolver` port (`secret://...`), so a config dump cannot leak them. The one
# exception is `WEBHOOK_SECRETS` at the bottom of this file, which exists because no secret manager
# is integrated yet — the *port* is what matters, and swapping in a real resolver changes one file.

# --- process ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# development | test | production
NODE_ENV=development
# web | worker | all — which half of the system this process runs.
#   web    — the HTTP listener only (webhook ingest and health).
#   worker — the poll loops, the reaper timer and the outbox relay timer. No socket.
#   all    — both, in one process. Convenient locally; separate them to scale them separately.
APP_ROLE=all
# debug | info | warn | error
LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- outbound safety ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SIMULATE | LIVE
#
# STILL SIMULATE, and LIVE is STILL REFUSED AT BOOT. Phase 02B connects real Meta inbound
# transport in receive-only mode; it builds no provider *send* transport at all. There is no
# outbound HTTP client in src/ and no Meta API base URL, which is asserted by
# tests/architecture/no-outbound-meta.test.ts. Setting LIVE throws OUTBOUND_LIVE_NOT_AUTHORIZED.
#
# Note that this flag is not the only brake, and not the important one. Every channel connection
# carries its own `transport_mode`, which provisioning always writes as RECEIVE_ONLY and which a
# database CHECK constraint refuses to pair with a send capability. A receive-only connection
# records SIMULATED outcomes even on a process that has somehow been armed.
OUTBOUND_MODE=SIMULATE

# --- durable job queue (ADR-004) ------------------------------------------------------------------
# How long a worker holds a job before the reaper may reclaim it. Must exceed the slowest handler,
# or a live handler and a reclaiming worker end up writing to the same conversation.
WORKER_LEASE_MS=60000
# Poll interval for claiming ready jobs. Polling is the correctness mechanism; LISTEN/NOTIFY is a
# Phase 1 latency optimisation, not a replacement.
WORKER_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=1000
# Bounded retry. On exhaustion a job is dead-lettered, recorded, and — for moderator work — a
# human handoff is raised. There is no unbounded retry path.
WORKER_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
# How many independent poll loops this process runs. Each claims on its own; partition
# exclusivity is what keeps them from colliding, not coordination between them.
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=2
# How long a shutdown waits for in-flight work. Within it, a job either finishes or is aborted and
# its lease handed straight back. Past it the lease is left to expire and the reaper recovers it —
# slower, but it cannot put two writers on one conversation. Keep this below your orchestrator's
# kill timeout: a shutdown that outlives SIGKILL is not graceful, it is a slow crash.
WORKER_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS=15000

# --- background timers ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The reaper. Crash recovery that nobody triggers is crash recovery that does not happen — this is
# the timer that closes the oldest open risk in the project.
REAPER_INTERVAL_MS=10000
# The outbox relay. Cross-tenant by nature, so it runs on a timer rather than as a queued job.
RELAY_INTERVAL_MS=5000
RELAY_BATCH_SIZE=100

# --- http ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HTTP_PORT=8080
HTTP_HOST=0.0.0.0
# Slow-loris bounds. Without them a handful of connections that dribble headers hold the listener.
HTTP_HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
HTTP_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
# How long in-flight requests may finish after a signal before the listener force-closes.
HTTP_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS=10000

# --- health -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Beyond these ages a backlog is a degradation rather than normal queue latency.
HEALTH_INBOUND_LAG_MS=300000
HEALTH_OUTBOX_LAG_MS=300000
# Any dead letter at all is an alert: this is the one queue that should be empty. Raising it is a
# deliberate act.
HEALTH_DEAD_LETTER_CEILING=0

# --- language model (ADR-007) ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Still `mock` only. No real model is connected and no network call is made anywhere in this phase.
LLM_PROVIDER=mock
LLM_REASON_MODEL=mock-model-1
LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
LLM_TEMPERATURE=0.2
LLM_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=1024

# --- ingest -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hard cap on a webhook body, enforced before parsing. Oversized bodies are rejected, not buffered.
INGEST_MAX_BODY_BYTES=1000000

# --- database: four credentials, and a bootstrap that creates them --------------------------------
#
# The schema separates *privilege roles* from *login identities*. Privilege roles hold the grants
# and are NOLOGIN — `app_migrator`, `app_runtime`, `app_worker`, `app_relay` can never appear in a
# connection string. Login identities hold the passwords and no privileges of their own beyond
# membership, which is what lets a credential be rotated without touching a single GRANT.
#
#   app_runtime_login   -> app_runtime   RLS-bound. Acts only inside SET LOCAL app.account_id.
#   app_worker_login    -> app_worker    The queue across tenants. `jobs` and `dead_letters` only.
#   app_relay_login     -> app_relay     The outbox across tenants. SELECT, and UPDATE on
#                                        `relayed_at` alone.
#   app_migrator_login  -> app_migrator  Owns and changes the schema. BYPASSRLS. NEVER serves
#                                        traffic, and never belongs in this file.
#
# The three below are required by `npm start`. They are separate on purpose and there is
# deliberately no single-URL fallback: collapsing them would quietly merge three privilege
# boundaries into one.
# PG_RUNTIME_URL=postgres://app_runtime_login:password@localhost:5432/all_in_moderation
# PG_WORKER_URL=postgres://app_worker_login:password@localhost:5432/all_in_moderation
# PG_RELAY_URL=postgres://app_relay_login:password@localhost:5432/all_in_moderation

# --- deploy-time only: never in the running process's environment --------------------------------
#
# None of the following is read by `npm start`. They belong to one-off commands run by a human, and
# a service that holds them is a service that can rewrite or drop its own schema.
#
#   PG_BOOTSTRAP_URL      A privileged connection — superuser, or an owner with CREATEROLE and
#                         BYPASSRLS. Used by `src/runtime/cli/bootstrap.ts` and by nothing else, to
#                         create the eight roles above, grant the memberships, install pgcrypto and
#                         grant the migrator CREATE on schema public. Idempotent; it will not reset
#                         a password unless you pass --rotate-passwords.
#
#   PG_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD  Consulted by the bootstrap only when an identity must be created (or
#   PG_RUNTIME_PASSWORD   rotated). Generate them: openssl rand -hex 24. Or pipe all four as JSON:
#   PG_WORKER_PASSWORD      ... | node src/runtime/cli/bootstrap.ts --passwords-stdin
#   PG_RELAY_PASSWORD     Passwords are never accepted as command-line arguments; --password is
#                         refused, because argv is readable by every process on the host.
#
#   PG_MIGRATOR_URL       The migrator *identity* — app_migrator_login, not app_migrator, which is
#                         NOLOGIN and cannot connect at all. `src/runtime/cli/migrate.ts` uses it to
#                         SET ROLE app_migrator for the length of the run: object ownership and
#                         BYPASSRLS both come from that SET ROLE, because PostgreSQL does not
#                         inherit role attributes through membership.
#
#   PG_MIGRATOR_ROLE      Optional. The privilege role to assume; defaults to `app_migrator`. Only
#                         set it if you bootstrapped under different names.
#
# The whole procedure, from an empty cluster to a running service, with the managed-PostgreSQL
# capability requirements spelled out:  docs/DEPLOYMENT-BOOTSTRAP.md

# --- webhook secrets ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# JSON, keyed by the `credentials_ref` on the channel connection row:
#
#   {"secret://meta/acme": {"appSecret": "...", "verifyToken": "..."}}
#
# `appSecret` verifies the HMAC over every delivery's raw body. `verifyToken` answers the
# provider's one-time subscription handshake; a connection with no verify token refuses the
# handshake rather than accepting any token. A stop-gap until a secret manager is integrated —
# the resolver is a port, and this is one implementation of it.
#
# There is deliberately NO page/Instagram/WhatsApp access token here, and no field for one. A token
# is what would authorise sending, and this phase has nothing that could use one; provisioning
# would ignore it, and its presence in an environment file would be a credential with no purpose
# and a long tail.
#
# That is a statement about the *runtime*, not about the whole procedure. Subscribing a Page or a
# WhatsApp Business Account to the app is an authenticated Graph API write, so an operator does
# need a send-capable token in their own hands, for the length of one call, at that one step. It
# stays in their shell or in the Graph API Explorer and never reaches this file, this process or
# the database. The three kinds of credential are separated by name in
# docs/META-RECEIVE-ONLY-OPERATOR-CHECKLIST.md §3.
#
# Provisioning refuses a `credentials_ref` that does not resolve to BOTH halves, so a
# misconfigured bundle fails at `meta-connection provision` rather than as a signature failure
# against real traffic. See docs/META-RECEIVE-ONLY-OPERATOR-CHECKLIST.md.
# WEBHOOK_SECRETS={}

# --- attaching a real Meta connection -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Nothing above is enough on its own. A real Facebook Page, Instagram professional account or
# WhatsApp number needs an operator to create a Meta app, expose a public HTTPS callback, complete
# the subscription handshake and subscribe the surface's fields. Every step, in order, with the
# exact permissions to request and the ones to deliberately avoid:
#
#     docs/META-RECEIVE-ONLY-OPERATOR-CHECKLIST.md
#
# And before any of it, the database has to exist with the four credentials above. An empty
# PostgreSQL cluster has none of them, and the privilege roles the migrations create are NOLOGIN:
#
#     docs/DEPLOYMENT-BOOTSTRAP.md
#
# Provision the connection first — it generates the webhook path Meta must be pointed at:
#
#   node src/runtime/cli/meta-connection.ts provision --account acc_... --channel FACEBOOK \
#     --external-id <page id> --credentials-ref secret://<client>/meta
#
# Then prove the chain, with no Meta account required:
#
#   node src/runtime/cli/shadow-acceptance.ts --self-test
#
# And against a real connection once one is attached:
#
#   node src/runtime/cli/meta-connection.ts status --account acc_... --connection chc_...
#   node src/runtime/cli/shadow-acceptance.ts --account acc_... --connection chc_...
